  push    dx      ; Save DX (mul zaps it)
  dec     ax      ; Normalise index
  add     ax,ax
  mul     cx      ; First dimension
  dec     bx      ; Normalise index
  add     bx,bx
  add     ax,bx   ; Second dimension
  pop     bx      ; Retrieve DX in BX
  add     bx,ax   ; Address of element
  mov     ax,[bx] ; Get element in AX
  retf

So supposing an array foo%(m%*n%) i.e. m% x n% array

  value%=usr(addr(mc%(1)), i%, j%, n%, addr(foo%(1)))

should retrieve the element foo%(i%, j%) where 1<=i%<=m% and 1<=j%<=n%

If this is right then mc%(9) is:

mc%(1)=$4852
mc%(2)=$C003
mc%(3)=$E1F7
mc%(4)=$034B
mc%(5)=$03DB
mc%(6)=$5BC3
mc%(7)=$D803
mc%(8)=$078B
mc%(9)=$00CB

I've given this a quick test and it seems okay.  It would be faster to 
pass the addr() stuff as variables in the usr() function, of course. I 
don't know what the overhead of usr() itself is, though.

David, a rainy afternoon, Edinburgh


